I'm not doing a very good job.

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:15.650
Having an espresso machine
right next to my desk

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:18.390
is very dangerous and
probably a stupid idea.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:19.790
So you know what's pretty crazy?

00:00:19.790 --> 00:00:21.350
The human brain is pretty crazy.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:22.980
It's such a fascinating machine,

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.050
and we barely have scratched the surface

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:26.140
of what it's capable of.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:27.440
But for that very reason,

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:29.710
we don't really understand it fully.

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:33.120
Like why is it that some days
it seems like our friend,

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.720
like we're full of
confidence, full of energy,

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.300
witty, sharp as a tack,

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:39.620
while other days it feels like our enemy.

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.530
You know, you feel
lethargic, you feel sluggish.

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:45.420
It feels like someone just
poured over a wad of molasses

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.284
over your brain, and
it's just slowing down

00:00:47.284 --> 00:00:49.000
all the gears of your mind.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.730
And on these days, it seems
to completely sabotage

00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:55.270
our ability to focus or
remember in the short term,

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.660
or even the long term.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:58.990
Basically, it just screws
over our brain completely.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:02.990
This dilapidated state is
often referred to as brain fog,

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:04.890
and, according to the internet,

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.820
is characterized primarily
by a lack of mental clarity,

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:12.160
inability to focus and
a decreased function

00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:13.140
of working memory.

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.730
But why does this happen
and how can we prevent it?

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:16.940
Over the past couple of years,

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:18.850
I've been trying to answer this question.

00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:20.470
Because brain fog sucks,

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:23.220
especially, if you have an
important meeting or a date,

00:01:23.220 --> 00:01:25.810
or you're interacting with
people a lot of the time,

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.160
or you have to solve a problem.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:29.090
A lot of days you can't afford to be

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.480
in this state of brain fog.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.300
You're presenting to the
world like the worst possible

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:34.860
version of yourself, and it's annoying.

00:01:34.860 --> 00:01:36.170
So in this video, we're gonna go over

00:01:36.170 --> 00:01:40.340
three powerful strategies to
clear up that mental bleh.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:42.130
We're gonna go over three reasons

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:45.180
why you probably have brain
fog and how to fix them,

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.040
how to fix them,

00:01:46.040 --> 00:01:48.027
how to fix them, how to catch them all.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:52.250
Factor number one is overstimulation.

00:01:52.250 --> 00:01:54.050
I think it was Tim Ferriss who said

00:01:54.050 --> 00:01:56.330
that we live in a digital environment

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:57.930
where attention is currency.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:01:59.120
All of these different companies,

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.480
all these different
websites, these advertisers,

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.060
they want your attention
because they can monetize it.

00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:06.380
Whenever you click on
something, somebody gets paid.

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:08.520
Whenever you watch something,
somebody gets paid.

